---
description: Checks and tests implement the design's full statement, not a necessary condition of it
alwaysApply: true
---

# Checking the design's statements

A check or a test that stands for a statement in `docs/design.ja.md` implements the whole statement, not a necessary condition of it.

- Every documented contract has a code path and a test. A sentence with no code behind it is a defect.
- Before the PR, list each contract sentence the PR adds to `docs/design.ja.md` next to the test that checks it. Sentences a PR writes itself (an order of a queue, a count taken at the call, each case of a method, a continuation that throws) are the ones most often left with no test.
- Each override a PR adds to a family of classes (a plan's `Shape` or `SelectRows`, a geom's recording) is reached by a test: search the tests for an input that builds that class, not one that only holds the column it would read. PR #369 added the selection to the category x's bars, error bars, and stack; its test table had a category column but called `Bar` and `ErrorBar` with the numeric x, so none of the three ran until the review asked.
- A trigger that decides whether a change is passed on compares the fields the contract names, not the whole value: a link that copies the primary x and y queues a change of the primary ranges alone. PR #379 queued a change of a secondary axis too, so a member's constraint ran on a copy that changed nothing; the review found it.
- An API that runs the caller's code (an event handler, a data source callback, a custom formatter, a hit-test callback) is tested with that code doing what it may do to the same subsystem: change the spec or the data it is called for, remove itself, add another of its kind, throw. A callback that throws must not lose the other work of the same pass.
- A walk up a tree (layers, facets, the spec's nesting) stops at the root the tree names, not at the first node without a parent: nodes are built before they are attached.
- A rejection in `docs/design-decisions.md` whose reason is behavior ("an aggregation per frame over the raw data misses the budget at 10^9 points") has a test or a benchmark that fails when the rejected option is put in its place.
- When the design fixes an order (the frame stages, the resize steps, the disposal order, the error priority), test it with one input that fails two adjacent steps and assert the first.
- When the design fixes a state machine (a data source's load states, a worker job's states, a device's lost and restored states), test every transition it lists and one transition it does not allow.
- A test seam replaces every source of the values the contract reports, not only the call that fails. A seam that returns a device-lost HRESULT leaves the device alive, so `GetDeviceRemovedReason` gives `S_OK`, and a test of `RemovedReason` checks nothing unless the seam replaces the reason too (PR #35).
- A test of an invariant (§11) includes the input that would break it: a stale handle, a chunk read after its file is unmapped, a version read from a worker that has moved on, a call from the wrong thread.
- A statement about what a spec draws ("a plot of no layers draws nothing") is tested through the render, not only on the spec the builder makes. PR #82 checked that `Build` gave no layers, and the review asked for the render.
- A statement about the final output (the pixel a point lands on) is tested at that output, not only at the value that decides it upstream (M4's X). PR #89 first checked the X and said the pixel followed from it; the review asked for the pixels.
- A fixed-size buffer on a path every frame takes has a test above its capacity.
- A change that gives the elements of one layer different looks (a color an element, a color a stacked group) is checked in each coordinate system's recording path. Polar recorded a layer's rectangles as one shape in its last color, so row G7c's bars colored by a number were one color in polar until PR #211 found it with a stacked polar scene.
- A comparison removed from a key as the same as another is shown to have no input where the two differ, or it stays.
- When a statement lists the values a mechanism covers (column types, mark kinds, scale kinds), each value has a test on an input where the other values' paths do not run.
- A change that adds a new kind of input to a channel (a raster as the color column, a new column type for x) checks each statement the design makes about that channel's input again for the new kind, with a test each. PR #201 made a raster of numbers the color column and missed §4.2's "under a time scale the color column is `long`": a `float` raster passed, and the review found it.
- A value a factory refuses (`WithSecondaryAxis` on a time scale) is refused by the compile too, when a spec puts it in with `with`. PR #235 refused it in the factory only, and the review found the way round it.
- A test row that names what a recording draws (an axis line's center, a tick's direction) checks the polylines, not only the texts. PR #235's test of the top and right axes read the labels and the titles only, and the review asked for the lines and the ticks.
- A layer colored by its own values (an image of numbers, 2D bins) is checked in every guide that lists layers: the legend gives it no row of its own, its color bar stands for it. PR #227's 2D bins with a `Label` drew a row in the palette's color, unrelated to the cells, and the review found it; the legend's two places that chose the rows now share one function.
- When a design statement changes, search `docs/`, the READMEs, `.cursor/rules/`, and XML docs for every restatement and update them in the same PR.
- A result that the design matches to a reference (M4's equivalence with drawing the raw line, a tick algorithm, a color scale's interpolation, the density of a scatter against counting the points) is tested at the reference's corner cases: NaN and infinities in the data, a value past the scale's range, a pixel column with one point and one with none, a chunk boundary inside a pixel column, the last partial chunk, a viewport smaller than one bucket, and the input where the cases of a formula meet.
- A claim that two results are the same says how far, as the test compares them: by value, or bit for bit. PR #31 wrote "bit for bit" for a test that compared the minimum and maximum by value, where -0 equals +0 and the vector minimum can differ in the sign of a zero.
- A relation the design states between two outputs (one record equals another with x and y swapped, one image equals another mirrored) is computed on a few points before it goes in, from the formulas that make each output. PR #137 wrote that a flipped record equals the unflipped one with x and y swapped; screen y points down, so the two are point-symmetric about the panel's center, and the review found it with four points. The relation holds when both scales are also reversed.
- Before a PR says "every X does Y" (every mark kind has a large-data path, every GPU resource is released on device loss), search the whole solution for X. The PR's test plan names the search and the exceptions.
- A statement a PR adds to a row of design §10 is a test in the same PR. PR #342 added "a layer not drawn yet answers nothing" and "a figure answers in the plot under the cursor" to row I2a and went to review without a test of either.
- A number composed across parts (a base times a width plus an index) is tested with parts of different widths side by side. Row I2a numbered a stack's groups by each part's own width, an area of 1 below bands of 2, and the band's high edge and the next group's area met; its tests had parts of one width only, and PR #352's review of the markers' colors found it.
- That test also lists every key that tells two parts apart (a group, a side, an edge), and includes a pair of parts that differ in one key alone. PR #360 numbered a numeric stack's bars by their group, and a group's positive and negative pieces took the same number; the review found it.
- A statement that something stays through a change of the view (a measurement kept in the scale's space) is tested on every part drawn from it (the line, the fill, the box of text), after a zoom. Each part's place is computed from the state at each draw: a position in physical pixels kept from the time the state was set is stale after the first zoom or pan. PR #395 kept the anchor of the measurement's box in pixels from the measuring, its test read the line alone after the zoom, and the review found the box left behind.

A test of a boundary uses data whose value is the boundary, not a call of the helper that decides it: PR #415 first tested the widths of a density tile with tiles of 100, 65,000, and 66,000 points and called the width function for 255 and 256, so a tile packed with a rule of its own would have passed. A test whose data is named for a shape (a capped leaf, a split cell below a level) asserts that the shape is in the data: the first remark of PR #415's test said that the nested points had split cells below level 24, and a mutant showed that they had none.

```text
BAD:  "M4 keeps the first, last, minimum, and maximum point of each pixel column"
      → test checks that the drawn line reaches the minimum and maximum
GOOD: data where the first and last points differ from the minimum and maximum in every column, plus a column with
      one point and an empty one; assert the exact points kept for each column, in order
```
